# Run when labwc starts.
#
# UPSTREAM SHIPS AN autostart EXAMPLE TO share/doc/labwc/ AND INSTALLS NOTHING.
# Without this file the session starts bare: nothing but whatever the user
# launches by hand.
#
# THE HELPERS ARE NOT HERE ANY MORE, AND THE ABSENCE IS THE FIX. swaybg and
# yambar used to be launched from this file, and cleanup of a previous
# session's copies was a pkill in labwc-session -- which races the old
# session's teardown, so anything not yet running when the pkill fired
# survived it. Verified on hardware: two yambars after a restart, with the
# pkill demonstrably present in the image. They are dinit services now
# (etc/dinit.d/yambar, etc/dinit.d/swaybg): `depends-on = labwc` gives their
# lifetime an owner, `restart = true` replaces the supervision loop this
# file carried, and scripts/wayland-app waits for the compositor's socket,
# which is the readiness this file used to get for free by running inside
# the session. Launching them here AS WELL would recreate the duplicate.
#
# WHAT REMAINS HERE IS WHAT MUST RUN INSIDE THE SESSION, ONCE, AT ITS START.

# THE BOOT GATE. tty1 autologins straight to the desktop, so without this
# anyone who powers the machine on is in; the lock screen protected only a
# session someone had already locked. If the person has armed the gate --
# `veron-enroll autologin off`, which refuses unless an enrolled factor
# demonstrably unwraps -- the session starts locked, behind the same
# ext-session-lock-v1 surface and the same factors as Super+L.
#
# THE DEFAULT IS OPEN, AND MUST STAY OPEN: the live image is a demo that
# must not demand a key file the user has not got, and a fresh install has
# no factors yet. `autologin=off` can only be written by an enrolment that
# proved a factor works, so the exact-match grep below fails safe in every
# other state: no config, no line, or `on`.
#
# FAILURE FAILS LOCKED, NOT OPEN, AND THAT IS THE PROTOCOL'S PROPERTY
# RATHER THAN THIS FILE'S: the compositor holds the lock whether or not
# veron-lock survives, so a gate that crashes strands the GUI locked --
# better than a display manager that segfaults to a shell. Ctrl+Alt+F2
# stays reachable as the escape hatch, and veron-login guards tty3.
if [ -x /usr/bin/veron-lock ] \
   && grep -qx 'autologin=off' "$HOME/.config/veron/auth.conf" 2>/dev/null
then
    # THE FACTOR LIVES ON REMOVABLE MEDIA, AND NOTHING ELSE MOUNTS IT AT
    # BOOT -- measured on the first armed boot: the gate came up, the stick
    # was in, and every Enter was refused because the key file's PATH did
    # not exist until a hand-run `dinitctl start media` from the spare
    # console. So the gate mounts it first: veron is in `power`, the socket
    # is group-writable by now, and a failure (no stick inserted) leaves
    # the lock up and refusing -- the correct state -- until the person
    # inserts it and `dinitctl start media` from Ctrl+Alt+F2, which stays
    # the documented way in.
    /usr/bin/dinitctl -p /run/dinitctl start media 2>/dev/null || true
    /usr/bin/veron-lock &
fi
